===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sterh₃-}}
From Anglian {{inh|en|ang|strēt|t=street}} (cognate West Saxon form {{m|ang|strǣt}}) from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*strātu|t=street}}, an early borrowing from {{der|en|LL.|-}} ({{m|la|via}}) {{m|la|strāta||paved (road)}}, from {{der|en|la|strātus}}, past participle of {{m|la|sternō||stretch out, spread, bestrew with, cover, pave}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*sterh₃-||to stretch out, extend, spread}}. 

Cognate with {{cog|sco|stret}}, {{m|sco|strete}}, {{m|sco|streit||street}}, {{cog|stq|Sträite||street}}, {{cog|fy|strjitte||street}}, {{cog|nl|straat||street}}, {{cog|nds-de|Straat||street}}, {{cog|de|Straße||street}}, {{cog|sv|stråt||way, path}}, {{cog|is|stræti||street}} (Scandinavian forms are borrowed from Old English), Portuguese {{m|pt|estrada||road, way, drive}}, Italian {{m|it|strada||road, street}}. Related to {{cog|ang|strēowian}}, {{m|ang|strewian||to strew, scatter}}, {{cog|la|sternō}}, Ancient {{cog|el|στορνύναι}}. More at {{l|en|strew}}.

The {{IPAchar|/aː/}} vowel of the Latin form shifted by [[w:Anglo-Frisian brightening|Anglo-Frisian brightening]] to {{IPAchar|/æː/}} in West Saxon and {{IPAchar|/eː/}} in Anglian Old English; these developed respectively to {{IPAchar|/ɛː/}} and {{IPAchar|/eː/}} in Middle English, {{IPAchar|/eː/}} and {{IPAchar|/iː/}}  in Early Modern English, and finally {{IPAchar|/iː/}} in Modern English by the {{w|Great Vowel Shift}}. The modern spelling reflects the Anglian form, as in {{m|en|sleep}}, {{m|en|greedy}}, {{m|en|sheep}}.

